title: Next-version computation collides with 2014 tag 0.001
updated: 2026-09-18T16:18:31Z
---

Both forced dzil test runs (system and share) log RewriteVersion::Transitional falling back to Git::NextVersion, then build version 0.001 -- the same number as the existing annotated tag 0.001 (tagger message v0.001, dated 2014-09-19, from a real past release).

Root cause: tag_format is the @Author::GETTY default of a bare version with no leading v, but Git::NextVersion's version_regexp defaults to requiring a leading v on the tag, and nothing in dist.ini or the GETTY / Git::VersionManager bundles overrides it to match. Confirmed by reading PluginBundle::Author::GETTY (only sets Git::Tag tag_format) and PluginBundle::Git::VersionManager (does not propagate tag_format into the fallback version provider).

Effect: Git::NextVersion cannot see the 0.001 tag as a version at all, so it falls back to first_version 0.001 again. Its own before_release duplicate-version check uses the same broken regexp, so it will not catch this. Git::Tag's exact-tag-name duplicate check should catch it and abort, but only after build, test, and possibly a CPAN upload has already run, depending on plugin order.

Rest of this pre-release audit is clean: cpanfile has Alien::Base as the runtime dep; dist.ini alien_repo, alien_bins and copyright_year are current; alien_bins and the POD tool set now agree (msgmerge included in both); Changes' unreleased section covers the diff since the 0.001 tag. This version-number collision is the one release-blocking item found.

Suggested fix direction, not applied here, a dist.ini and versioning change for alien-gettext-worker: give Git::NextVersion a version_regexp matching the bare tag format, or pass an explicit V override for the intended next version when releasing. Verify by re-running dzil test and checking that the build-version line no longer says 0.001.

